+.TH LLAPI_SCAN_CHANGELOG 3 2026-08-25 "Lustre User API" "Lustre Library Functions"
+.SH NAME
+llapi_scan_changelog \- read a Lustre Changelog as an Object Stream
+.SH SYNOPSIS
+.nf
+.B #include <lustre/lustreapi.h>
+.PP
+.BI "typedef int (*llapi_scan_cb_t)(const struct llapi_scan_rec *" rec ,
+.BI "                               void *" data ");"
+.PP
+.BI "int llapi_scan_changelog(const struct llapi_scan_changelog_param *" sc ,
+.BI "                         llapi_scan_cb_t " cb ", void *" data ");"
+.PP
+.fi
+.SH DESCRIPTION
+.B llapi_scan_changelog()
+reads one MDT's Changelog and calls
+.I cb
+once per event, or with
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_COALESCE
+once per object, with the same
+.B struct llapi_scan_rec
+that
+.BR llapi_scan_namespace (3)
+and
+.BR llapi_scan_device (3)
+deliver. A consumer written for either of those is fed by this one without
+being rewritten.
+.LP
+.B It is not a scan.
+A Changelog answers what changed, never what exists: an object that has not
+been touched does not appear, however well it matches. Its window is also
+finite \(em records exist between the oldest unpurged index and now \(em so an
+answer from it means something only against an anchor, which is what a full
+scan provides.
+.SS What the record carries
+A Changelog record has the FID, the parent FID, the name, the event and its
+time, and, where the server recorded them, the uid and gid of whoever caused
+it. That pair is
+.I lfsr_event_uid
+and
+.IR lfsr_event_gid ,
+gated by
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_EVENT_UID ,
+and it is not
+.IR lfsr_stx.stx_uid :
+the MDT fills the Changelog's uidgid extension from the acting client's
+credentials, so it says who acted and not who owns the object, which is what
+.I lfsr_stx.stx_uid
+means in every scanner that fills it. Only a resolve can answer the owner.
+.LP
+A record has
+.B no
+size, blocks, mode, nlink, owner, object times, project id, layout, directory
+stripe or HSM state at any setting. Those fields arrive absent, with their
+.I lfsr_valid
+bit clear.
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_RESOLVE
+fills the first six of them \(em what one
+.BR fstat (2)
+on the object answers for. The project id, the layout, the directory stripe
+and the HSM state are not filled at any setting: each costs an ioctl or an
+xattr of its own, which a log this size cannot pay per record. A consumer
+that needs them holds the FID and can ask for them itself.
+.LP
+The event's own fields are
+.IR lfsr_event_type ,
+.I lfsr_event_flags
+(the
+.B CLF_FLAGMASK
+bits only \(em
+.BR CLF_RENAME ,
+.B CLF_JOBID
+and
+.B CLF_EXTRA_FLAGS
+say which extensions the record carries, not what happened, and are
+masked off),
+.I lfsr_event_time
+and
+.IR lfsr_event_index ;
+they are valid when
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_EVENT
+is set. A rename also carries
+.IR lfsr_src_fid ,
+.I lfsr_src_parent_fid
+and
+.IR lfsr_src_name ,
+gated by
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_EVENT_SRC ,
+and
+.I lfsr_jobid
+by
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_JOBID ,
+and the acting uid and gid by
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_EVENT_UID .
+.LP
+A
+.B CL_RENAME
+is about the object that moved, whose FID the Changelog carries in its rename
+extension, so
+.I lfsr_fid
+and
+.I lfsr_src_fid
+both name it.
+.B mv a b
+over an existing
+.I b
+is also about the object
+.I b
+named, which the rename replaced: it is delivered twice, once with
+.I lfsr_fid
+= that object and once with
+.I lfsr_fid
+= the one that moved, in event mode as in object mode. A
+.B CL_MIGRATE
+record also carries a rename extension, but its
+.I cr_sfid
+is the migrated object's old FID, which no longer exists: that record is
+delivered once, under the new FID.
+.LP
+.I sc_stats
+is filled in when the scan ends, and not every field means here what it
+means for a scan of a namespace or a target.
+.I ss_seen
+counts Changelog records examined and
+.I ss_emitted
+counts what was delivered \(em objects while coalescing, events otherwise \(em
+and
+.I ss_skipped
+and
+.I ss_class
+are not filled at all, a Changelog record carrying no class. The identity
+.B struct llapi_scan_stats
+documents between them does not hold for this scanner.
+.I lfsr_parent_fd
+and
+.I lfsr_fd
+are always \-1: this reader holds no descriptor.
+.LP
+Two fields are answered from the event itself.
+.I lfsr_event_time
+is when the event happened, in whole seconds since the epoch, and is
+deliberately not
+.IR lfsr_stx.stx_mtime :
+an event time and a modification time are different facts. And an event that
+implies a type decides it \(em
+.B CL_CREATE
+is a regular file,
+.B CL_MKDIR
+a directory,
+.B CL_SOFTLINK
+a symlink, and
+.B CL_RMDIR
+names a directory too \(em so
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_TYPE
+is set for those and clear for everything else. An object's type does not
+change, so the answer stands for every later event on it.
+.SS The parameters
+.TP
+.I sc_size
+.BR sizeof (*sc).
+Required, and how the structure grows.
+.TP
+.I sc_mdtname
+The target, as
+.BR testfs-MDT0000 .
+Read through the character device
+.BI /dev/changelog- mdtname
+that the client's
+.B mdc
+creates, so this runs on a client and needs no server access.
+.TP
+.I sc_user
+The registered Changelog user, as
+.BR cl1 .
+Required by
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_CLEAR .
+Registering one is an MDS-side operation and is the caller's:
+.B lctl --device
+.I mdtname
+.BR changelog_register .
+To use
+.IR sc_type_mask ,
+register the user with a mask or a name
+.RB ( "changelog_register -m ALL" ).
+On an MDS without the LU-20647 fix, a plain registration writes a record
+the MDS cannot look up, and the scan then fails with
+.BR -ENOENT .
+.TP
+.I sc_flags
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_*
+bits, described below. An unknown bit is refused.
+.TP
+.I sc_mnt
+A client mount of the same filesystem, or NULL.
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_RESOLVE
+looks each object up through it and needs it.
+.TP
+.I sc_stats
+A
+.B struct llapi_scan_stats
+filled in when the scan ends, or NULL; see
+.BR llapi_scan_device (3).
+.TP
+.I sc_padding
+Must be zero.
+.TP
+.I sc_want
+The
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_*
+fields the consumer will read; 0 means everything. A consumer asking only for
+what an event carries gets no lookup at all, even with
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_RESOLVE
+set.
+.TP
+.I sc_filter
+Called before any lookup. Return 0 to deliver, 1 to skip this record,
+negative to stop the stream. A skipped record is counted in
+.I ss_filtered
+rather than
+.IR ss_emitted ,
+and counts as consumed for
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_CLEAR .
+.TP
+.IR sc_startrec ", " sc_endrec
+The range of Changelog indexes to read, both ends inclusive; 0 means the
+oldest surviving record and the end of the log. An index is per-MDT and is
+only meaningful with the MDT it came from. A job resuming from a saved index
+passes that index plus one, or reads the record it processed last time
+again.
+.TP
+.I sc_type_mask
+.BI BIT( CL_ *)
+of the events wanted, intersected with the registered user's own mask; 0
+means every type the log holds. The client asks the MDS for
+.IR sc_user 's
+mask, which needs an MDS of Lustre 2.17.0 or later, and drops the other
+records as it reads them; they are still read from the MDT. A mask is
+refused without
+.IR sc_user ,
+because there is no user's mask to intersect it with, and refused with
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_CLEAR ,
+because a clear purges the user's records of every type, including the ones
+the mask kept from the consumer. Keep the mask within the user's own: the
+client takes an empty intersection as no mask at all, and delivers every
+type.
+.I sc_filter
+filters without one.
+.TP
+.IR sc_min_age ", " sc_max_cached
+While coalescing: how long an object must be quiet before it is delivered,
+and how many objects may be held. Zero takes the defaults, 600 seconds and
+100000 objects.
+.IP
+An object that keeps changing is not held for ever: once six times
+.I sc_min_age
+has passed since its first event, it is delivered on its next one and held
+afresh from there. A file written without pause is then delivered about once
+every six
+.IR sc_min_age ,
+and the records behind it can be cleared.
+.IP
+The age is measured on the stream, not on the wall clock: the current time
+is taken from each record as it arrives, so quiet is measured by the event
+times of the records read, and the clock stands still while no record
+arrives. Under
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_FOLLOW
+a filesystem with no activity therefore holds the objects it last saw for
+as long as it stays quiet, and with
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_CLEAR
+as well the records behind them stay uncleared for the same duration. A
+consumer that must act within a bounded time reads with
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_FOLLOW
+clear and calls again.
+.SS sc_got
+.I sc_got
+points at a
+.B __u64
+the scan fills before the first record with the subset of
+.I sc_want
+it will answer for, or is NULL when the caller does not want to know. It is
+.I lfsp_got
+for this entry point \(em see
+.BR llapi_scan_device (3)
+\(em so one consumer can ask all three scanners the same question.
+.LP
+A changelog answers for less than either sibling. The event fields are always
+available; the object's own attributes are added only under
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_RESOLVE
+with
+.I sc_mnt
+naming a mount, since a lookup is what supplies them. A project id, a layout,
+an LMV, HSM state, a linkea, an inode number, a class and an OST object's
+owner are never filled at any setting, and
+.I sc_got
+is what says so before the scan runs rather than leaving a caller to infer it
+from records that never carry the field.
+.SS The flags
+.TP
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_FOLLOW
+Do not stop at the end of the log; wait for new records. With
+.I sc_type_mask
+as well, the range ends only when a record at or past
+.I sc_endrec
+passes the mask, since the others are dropped before they are read.
+.TP
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_COALESCE
+Deliver one record per object rather than one per event, once the object has
+been quiet for
+.IR sc_min_age .
+A file written a hundred times is then one record and one lookup.
+.IP
+The record carries the object's
+.B latest
+event: its
+.IR lfsr_event_index ", " lfsr_event_time " and " lfsr_event_type
+are that event's and not an earlier one's.
+.IP
+The fields an event does not always carry are the latest that
+.B did
+carry one, which is not the same thing. A Changelog record has a name only
+when the event had one to record \(em
+.BR mdd_changelog_data_store_by_fid ()
+writes none for
+.BR CL_CLOSE ", " CL_TRUNC ", " CL_SETATTR
+and their kind \(em so a file created, written and closed arrives as one
+record whose
+.I lfsr_event_type
+is
+.B CL_CLOSE
+while its
+.I lfsr_name
+is the one
+.B CL_CREATE
+recorded. Keeping it is the point: taking the latest event's would leave
+most coalesced records with no name at all.
+.IR lfsr_parent_fid ", " lfsr_jobid ", " lfsr_src_fid ", " lfsr_src_parent_fid ", "
+.IR lfsr_src_name " and " lfsr_event_uid
+follow the same rule.
+.IP
+So an object the log named more than once \(em created under one name and
+hardlinked under another \(em arrives carrying only the last of those names,
+and a consumer matching on
+.I lfsr_name
+sees one name per object rather than every name the log recorded for it.
+.TP
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_RESOLVE
+Fill what the Changelog does not carry, by opening each object by FID with
+.B O_PATH
+through
+.IR sc_mnt .
+One open per object, and a stat or two that ask the MDT again, so another
+client's change to the owner or mode is seen. A regular file's size and times
+are exact only when the demand mask is 0 or names a size, blocks or a time,
+which glimpses the OSTs; otherwise the size is the lazy one, marked by
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_LAZY_SIZE
+and
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_LAZY_BLOCKS ,
+and the times are left out. A lazy size or block count of 0 is not marked:
+with no size-on-MDT the client answers 0 for a file it has not glimpsed,
+which cannot be told from an empty file. The times keep their nanoseconds,
+and the birth time is filled where the MDT has one. No object is opened for read, so resolution adds
+no
+.B CL_OPEN
+or
+.B CL_CLOSE
+records to the log it reads. Resolution
+.B fills
+what the stream lacks; it never removes an object from the stream and never
+overrides what the event recorded, so the acting uid in
+.I lfsr_event_uid
+stays as recorded while
+.I lfsr_stx.stx_uid
+is filled with the owner the lookup found. An object that has since been
+unlinked cannot be opened, which is
+not an error: the record is delivered with what the event said and the rest
+absent.
+.TP
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_ONCE
+Deliver each object at most once for the whole stream, rather than once per
+burst. Requires
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_COALESCE ,
+and is refused with
+.B -EINVAL
+without it.
+.IP
+Coalescing reduces an object to one record only while it is held: the cache
+is a window, so an object touched again after it has aged out past
+.IR sc_min_age ,
+or after
+.I sc_max_cached
+pushed it out early, arrives a second time. A consumer that reports objects
+rather than events \(em
+.B lfs find --since
+is one \(em then reports it twice. This holds the FID of every object
+already delivered and drops the repeats.
+.IP
+.I sc_filter
+still sees them, before they are dropped, so a consumer anchoring on
+.I lfsr_event_index
+sees every record the stream produced. A dropped repeat is counted in
+.I ss_seen
+and not in
+.IR ss_emitted ,
+and the resolve a delivery would have cost is not paid.
+.IP
+The set holds one
+.B struct lu_fid
+per distinct object the log touched, in a table grown by doubling, and lives
+for the length of the call: memory in the size of the answer rather than of
+the log. A window wide enough to name tens of millions of objects costs
+hundreds of megabytes, which a consumer that cannot afford trades for
+duplicates by leaving this clear.
+.TP
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_CLEAR
+Purge consumed records as they are accepted, never ahead of the consumer. A
+record the consumer decided about is consumed whether
+.I cb
+took it or
+.I sc_filter
+rejected it: a filter is the consumer's own code reading the record, so a
+scan whose filter rejects everything still clears what it read. Object mode
+clears no further than the oldest record an object still held depends on.
+.B This cannot be undone
+and it purges the whole registered user's backlog, not this reader's alone,
+so a user id shared with another consumer loses what is cleared. Off by
+default.
+.SH RETURN VALUES
+.TP
+0
+on success.
+.TP
+>0
+the value a callback returned to stop the stream, unchanged.
+.TP
+<0
+a negative errno, or a negative value a consumer's
+.I cb
+returned to stop the stream, unchanged.
+.SH ERRORS
+.TP 15
+.B -EINVAL
+.I sc
+or
+.I cb
+is NULL;
+.I sc_mdtname
+is NULL;
+.I sc_size
+is outside the range this library accepts;
+.I sc_flags
+sets a bit it does not define;
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_RESOLVE
+without
+.IR sc_mnt ;
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_CLEAR
+without
+.IR sc_user ;
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_ONCE
+without
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_COALESCE ;
+.I sc_type_mask
+non-zero without
+.I sc_user
+\(em the mask is a property of a registered user, so there is nothing to
+apply it to;
+.I sc_type_mask
+non-zero with
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_CLEAR
+\(em the clear would purge records the mask never delivered;
+.I sc_padding
+non-zero, which is what keeps a field carved out of it later from being
+ignored without a word; or
+.I sc_stats
+is not NULL and its
+.I ss_size
+is smaller than the oldest
+.B struct llapi_scan_stats
+this library writes back.
+.TP 15
+.B -EXDEV
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_RESOLVE
+with an
+.I sc_mnt
+of another filesystem than the one
+.I sc_mdtname
+belongs to: a FID names nothing across filesystems, so the lookup would
+fill the record from an unrelated object.
+.TP 15
+.B -ENOENT
+no Changelog device for
+.IR sc_mdtname ,
+which usually means no client is mounted or the name is wrong. With
+.IR sc_type_mask ,
+it can also mean the MDS did not find
+.IR sc_user :
+see
+.I sc_user
+above.
+.TP 15
+.B -EACCES
+the Changelog device is not readable by this user.
+.SH NOTES
+.SS Registering without consuming fills the MDT
+A registered Changelog user is a promise to consume. Records accumulate for
+as long as the user stays registered and nothing clears them, and the cure is
+to consume, to clear, or to deregister with
+.B lctl --device
+.I mdtname
+.BR changelog_deregister .
+.SS Under DNE there is one Changelog per MDT
+Order within one MDT is exact, by
+.IR cr_index .
+Across MDTs there is no global order: an index is per-MDT, and
+.I cr_time
+is a wall clock on each MDS. A rename that moves an object between MDTs
+appears in both logs.
+.SS What the server records is not this reader's choice
+.B CHANGELOG_DEFMASK
+excludes
+.BR CL_ATIME ,
+.BR CL_OPEN ,
+.B CL_GETXATTR
+and
+.BR CL_DN_OPEN ,
+so on a default filesystem "recently accessed" is not a Changelog question at
+all. Enabling them is an administrator's decision, with a cost.
+.SH EXAMPLES
+To report every object that changed on one MDT since a known index, one
+record per object rather than one per event:
+.EX
+        static int changed_cb(const struct llapi_scan_rec *rec, void *data)
+        {
+                if (rec->lfsr_valid & LLAPI_SCAN_EVENT)
+                        printf("%llu " DFID " %s\en",
+                               (unsigned long long)rec->lfsr_event_index,
+                               PFID(&rec->lfsr_fid),
+                               rec->lfsr_name ? rec->lfsr_name : "");
+                return 0;
+        }
+\&
+        struct llapi_scan_changelog_param sc = { 0 };
+        struct llapi_scan_stats st = { .ss_size = sizeof(st) };
+\&
+        sc.sc_size = sizeof(sc);
+        sc.sc_mdtname = "testfs-MDT0000";
+        sc.sc_flags = LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_COALESCE;
+        sc.sc_startrec = last_index_this_job_saw + 1;
+        sc.sc_stats = &st;
+\&
+        rc = llapi_scan_changelog(&sc, changed_cb, NULL);
+.EE
+.LP
+.I lfsr_event_index
+is the checkpoint, with one caveat under
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_COALESCE .
+A record naming no object of its own is delivered as it arrives, while an
+object whose earliest record sits at a lower index is still held, so the
+indexes a consumer sees are not a watermark. A scan that reaches the end of
+the log delivers everything held before it closes, and the highest index
+seen is then safe to save. A scan the consumer
+.B stopped
+does not: what was held is dropped undelivered, and saving the highest index
+seen would step over it for good. A run that stopped must leave the
+checkpoint where it was.
+.LP
+Reading does not consume: the records stay until a registered user clears
+them, which is
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_CL_F_CLEAR
+with
+.IR sc_user ,
+or
+.BR lfs-changelog_clear (1).
+.SH AVAILABILITY
+.B llapi_scan_changelog()
+is part of the
+.BR lustre (7)
+user application interface library since release 2.18.0.
+.\" Added in commit v2_17_57-xxx-gxxxxxxxxx
+.SH SEE ALSO
+.BR lfs-changelog (1),
+.BR lfs-changelog_clear (1),
+.BR fstat (2),
+.BR statx (2),
+.BR llapi_changelog_clear (3),
+.BR llapi_changelog_start (3),
+.BR llapi_changelog_start_user (3),
+.BR llapi_scan_device (3),
+.BR llapi_scan_namespace (3),
+.BR llapi_scan_rec_path (3),
+.BR lustreapi (7)
